---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

/* collapse-header.css is imported once, globally, by Base.astro (every component page masthead
   consumes it); reveal and pulse are demoed only here, so they come in page-scoped like any other
   opt-in effect a consumer would import where it is used. */
import "@skryensya/core/effects/reveal.css";
import "@skryensya/core/effects/pulse.css";
import "../examples/effects.css";

const importReveal = `@import "@skryensya/core/effects/reveal.css";`;

const revealMarkup = `<div class="sk-fx-reveal">
  Appears the first time it scrolls into view.
</div>

<!-- A row that enters together: the second column waits a beat -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="sk-fx-reveal">One</div>
  <div class="sk-fx-reveal" style="--sk-fx-reveal-range: entry 18% cover 34%">Two</div>
</div>`;

const importCollapse = `@import "@skryensya/core/effects/collapse-header.css";`;

const collapseMarkup = `<div class="sk-fx-collapse-header__spacer"></div>

<header class="sk-fx-collapse-header">
  <h1>Page title</h1>
</header>`;

const collapseHooks = `.my-shell {
  --sk-fx-collapse-range: 96px;       /* scroll distance the collapse takes */
  --sk-fx-collapse-bar-height: 56px;  /* constant header height, collapsed or not */
  --sk-fx-collapse-top: 64px;         /* sticky offset: the height of whatever is already pinned above */
}`;

const importPulse = `@import "@skryensya/core/effects/pulse.css";`;

const pulseMarkup = `<span class="sk-badge sk-fx-pulse" data-tone="info">3 new</span>`;

const pulseReplay = `// Fire it again: drop the class, force a reflow, put it back.
el.classList.remove("sk-fx-pulse");
void el.offsetWidth;
el.classList.add("sk-fx-pulse");`;
---

<Base
  title="Effects"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="Opt-in CSS for decorative motion over an element or an already-published component: not a Contract."
>
  <h1>Effects</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An effect is <strong>a class added</strong> onto an element that already exists, whether that is
    your own markup or the root of a component the kit already ships. It is not a
    <a href="/en/architecture">Contract</a>: no signature, not compiled by the
    <code>ai-compiler</code>, not in the MCP. It is CSS, opt-in, built on the same
    <a href="/en/motion">motion intent tokens</a> every component already consumes.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
      <p>
        Why a fourth CSS tier instead of a Contract:
        <a href="/decisions/0020-effects-are-opt-in-css-over-motion-tokens">ADR-20</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What fails without this</h2>
  <p>
    A scroll reveal, a collapsing header, an emphasis pulse: each gets rewritten per project, its own
    <code>IntersectionObserver</code>, hand-picked durations, no <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.
    And once someone does pay attention to it, the result tends the other way: every block on the
    page slides in, and the effect that was meant to be an accent becomes the whole site's background
    noise.
  </p>

  <h2>The three rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Intent tokens only.</strong> Never a raw <code>--scale-*</code> primitive or a loose
      literal (<code>4px</code>, <code>200ms</code>): distance and timing come from
      <code>--motion-*</code>. Retuning the system moves the effect with it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Progressive.</strong> Anything scroll-driven lives behind
      <code>@supports (animation-timeline: …)</code> and
      <code>@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)</code>. Without support, the element stays
      visible, in its resting state: never hidden.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GPU only.</strong> <code>transform</code>, <code>opacity</code>, <code>translate</code>,
      short blur. Nothing that triggers layout.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="howto">
    And an editorial limit, not a technical one: the folder stays small on purpose. A catalogue of
    twenty effects is an invitation to decorate every corner, and a reader who sees the same motion a
    hundred times a day stops seeing it, or worse, starts avoiding it.
  </p>

  <h2>sk-fx-reveal</h2>
  <p>
    Fade + rise the first time an element scrolls into view, over
    <code>animation-timeline: view()</code>. No JavaScript: the browser decides when the element
    enters coverage and runs the animation once.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={importReveal} label="CSS" />
  <CodeBlock code={revealMarkup} label="HTML" />
  <p class="howto">
    Scroll the box: each row appears as it crosses the same point, not all at once.
  </p>
  <div class="fx-reveal-demo" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll reveal demo">
    <div class="fx-reveal-demo__spacer" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="fx-reveal-tile sk-fx-reveal">One</div>
    <div class="fx-reveal-tile sk-fx-reveal">Two</div>
    <div class="fx-reveal-tile sk-fx-reveal">Three</div>
    <div class="fx-reveal-tile sk-fx-reveal">Four</div>
  </div>
  <p class="howto">
    Not for everything. One or two moments that earn the gesture (a section heading, a figure, a card
    grid), not every block on the page: that is the seasickness the rule above exists to prevent.
  </p>

  <h2>sk-fx-collapse-header</h2>
  <p>
    A masthead that shrinks onto a fixed bar as the reader scrolls. The header's LAYOUT height never
    changes, it is always the small bar; what disappears is a separate spacer, exactly as tall as the
    collapse range, and the title simply renders larger than the bar measures, hanging over the top
    edge onto the spacer. It is the mechanism behind every component page's masthead
    (<a href="/en/components/accordion">scroll it there to see it live</a>), generalised in ADR-20.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={importCollapse} label="CSS" />
  <CodeBlock code={collapseMarkup} label="HTML" />
  <p>Three hooks the consumer declares on the ancestor wrapping both pieces:</p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header" scope="col">Hook</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header" scope="col">What it is</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">--sk-fx-collapse-range</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">
            The scroll distance the collapse takes. Has to be EXACTLY the spacer's rendered height:
            the two are the same number.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">--sk-fx-collapse-bar-height</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">The header's constant height, collapsed or not.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">--sk-fx-collapse-top</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">
            The sticky offset: whatever is already pinned above (a navbar, usually). 0 by default.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock code={collapseHooks} label="CSS" />
  <p class="howto">
    <code>--sk-fx-collapse-progress</code> is read-only: a registered <code>@property</code> that
    walks 0 to 1, so whoever paints above (a font-size, a padding, an eyebrow's opacity) interpolates
    with its own <code>calc()</code> instead of the effect dictating what changes.
  </p>

  <h2>sk-fx-pulse</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>scale</code> squeeze and back, twice, over <code>--motion-emphasize-*</code>: that
    intent already says "attention on a real change, use sparingly", and this effect is that
    property made into a class. It runs once when the class appears; there is no infinite variant, an
    element pulsing forever is exactly what this effect exists to replace.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={importPulse} label="CSS" />
  <CodeBlock code={pulseMarkup} label="HTML" />
  <div class="fx-pulse-demo">
    <span class="fx-pulse-dot sk-fx-pulse" data-fx-pulse-target aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span>3 new notifications</span>
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-variant="ghost" data-size="sm" type="button" data-fx-pulse-replay>
      Replay
    </button>
  </div>
  <p class="howto">To fire it again from code (a later change, not the first render):</p>
  <CodeBlock code={pulseReplay} label="JS" />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    No effect declares its own <code>@media (prefers-reduced-motion)</code> to turn itself off: they
    inherit the same mechanism every component does, the <a href="/en/motion">intent tokens</a>.
    <code>reveal</code> and <code>collapse-header</code> only run inside <code>no-preference</code>,
    so reduced motion leaves them in their resting state with no separate rule to keep in sync.
    <code>pulse</code> reads <code>--motion-emphasize-duration</code>, which already drops to 0 under
    reduced motion.
  </p>
</Base>

<script>
  import { initEffectsDemo } from "../scripts/effects-demo";

  initEffectsDemo();
</script>
